# AWE Phase Machine

## The 8 phases

| # | Phase | What happens | Human gate? |
|---|-------|--------------|-------------|
| 1 | `intake` | Ticket pulled (or pasted), sanitized into a structured spec, open questions collected in `plans/<ticket>/open-questions.md`. | Answers questions asynchronously by editing `open-questions.md`. |
| 2 | `architect` | Architect: root-grain graph → high-level `architecture.md`, gherkin E2E, `backend.spec.md` / `frontend.spec.md` (no file lists). Open questions optional. | Reads the spec; answers architect questions if any. |
| 3 | `approve` | `/awe-approve` validates any architect questions, then flips **specs** to `approved` after an explicit human **yes**. | **HARD GATE.** Nothing codes without this. |
| 4 | `code` | Per role (`backend` / `frontend`): branch `awe/<ticket>-<role>` (worktree **only** if another plan is already implementing), coder writes `implementation.plan.md` + `implementation-questions.md`, **stops if questions are open or `dependsOn` is unmet**, then TDD. Source writes are hook-blocked until questions are checked and dependencies are `done`. | Answers implementation questions. |
| 5 | `review` | Scanners run; `awe-reviewer` reviews the diff (optional `awe-security-reviewer` when `securityReview: true`); findings in `reviews/round-<N>.md`. `needs-fix` → respawn coder; `verified` → next phase. | Intervenes on any finding. |
| 6 | `verify` | `awe-verifier` writes `verification.md`: numbered human test steps (setup, URLs/commands, expected results, screenshots). | **HARD GATE.** Human tests by hand, then writes `verified: true` + initials + date. |
| 7 | `ship` | `/awe-ship` pre-flight (signoff + fresh evidence + clean scanners), commit, push `awe/<ticket>-*` branch, open PR via MCP or printed commands. Then a knowledge pass (`manage_adr`) if a lasting decision was made. | Merges the PR. |
| 8 | post-merge E2E | Combined end-to-end run on the base branch. Regressions re-enter via `/awe-regression` as `plans/<ticket>-R<N>/`. | Confirms production behavior. |

Phases are **per ticket**. Another ticket in `approve` does not freeze source writes for a ticket that is already in `code`.

## Allowed actions per phase (hard-enforced by hooks, per ticket)

| Phase | Write code | Write `plans/<ticket>/` | `git push` | Spawn dev subagent | Spawn reviewer |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| intake / architect / approve | ❌ hook denies | ✅ | ❌ | ❌ | ❌ |
| code | ✅ (role scope) | ✅ | ❌ | ✅ (approved plan only) | ✅ |
| review | ✅ (fixes) | ✅ | ❌ | ✅ (fix rounds) | ✅ |
| verify | ❌ (docs only by convention) | ✅ | ❌ | ❌ | ❌ |
| ship | ❌ | ✅ | ✅ (gated: signoff + evidence + branch) | ❌ | ❌ |

## State file — `.cursor/state/awe-state.json`

Only `/awe-*` skills (including `/awe-run`) write this file. Multiple tickets may be in-flight. Top-level `ticket`/`phase`/`roles` are the **session focus**; `tickets` is the source of truth. Legacy files with only the top-level fields still work (hooks synthesize a one-entry map).

```json
{
  "active": true,
  "ticket": "PROJ-123",
  "phase": "intake|architect|approve|code|review|verify|ship|done",
  "roles": {
    "backend":  { "planStatus": "draft|questions-open|approved", "iteration": 0, "verified": false },
    "frontend": { "planStatus": "draft|questions-open|approved", "iteration": 0, "verified": false }
  },
  "dependsOn": [],
  "tickets": {
    "PROJ-123": {
      "phase": "code",
      "roles": { "backend": { "planStatus": "approved", "iteration": 0, "verified": false } },
      "dependsOn": []
    },
    "PROJ-124": {
      "phase": "architect",
      "roles": { "frontend": { "planStatus": "draft", "iteration": 0, "verified": false } },
      "dependsOn": ["PROJ-123"]
    }
  },
  "updatedAt": "<ISO-8601>"
}
```

- `active: false` (or a missing file) means **AWE does not interfere at all** — every hook exits immediately. Shipping one ticket sets `active: false` only when no other ticket is still in-flight.
- `dependsOn` lists other plan ids. Intake is never blocked. **Application source** is denied until every listed ticket is `phase: done` (or has left state).
- `iteration` counts completed review rounds for that role on that ticket.
- `verified` flips true only on a reviewer's `verified` verdict.

## Evidence contract — `.cursor/state/awe-evidence.json`

Written by the coding agent after running the discovered or configured test command (`commands.test` from `.cursor/state/awe-discovered.json` or optional `awe.config.json`), and refreshed after every fix round:

```json
{ "testsPassed": true, "command": "npm test", "at": "<ISO-8601>" }
```

The `stop` hook demands evidence younger than **2 hours** before it lets a code/review session end. Stale or missing evidence forces the agent to re-run tests instead of claiming done.

This evidence is the machine-checkable slice of the standing **Definition of Done** — see `.cursor/skills/references/definition-of-done.md`. The DoD is the full bar (correctness, quality, integration, docs, ship-readiness); `awe-evidence.json` proves the Correctness slice (tests pass, run with the repo's own command, fresh), and the remaining slices are enforced by the review and ship gates. AWE never treats "tests pass" alone as done.

## Human signoff — `.cursor/state/awe-signoff.json`

Written by the human (or by the `/awe-verify` skill confirming the human's edit to `verification.md`):

```json
{ "verified": true, "initials": "MA", "at": "<ISO-8601>" }
```

`git push` in the ship phase is blocked until this exists with `verified: true`.

## Iteration budget semantics

`reviewIterations` (default 3) is a **hard budget per ticket**, not a suggestion. While `iteration < reviewIterations`, a `needs-fix` verdict respawns the coder with the findings appended to `handoff.md`. When the budget is reached without `verified`, the pipeline writes `plans/<ticket>/ESCALATION.md` and stops for a human decision. The budget exists so a stuck loop bothers a human instead of burning tokens.

## Orchestration

Default is **auto**: `/awe-run` (or a natural-language task that invokes it) chains intake → architect → code → review. APPROVE and VERIFY remain human stops. Individual `/awe-*` skills still work. `awe.config.json` is optional.
